{"type":"result","subtype":"success","session_id":"1385f7e9-86b5-4e16-ab58-a07ea1e33400","result":"## 🗂️ Report — windowed-runner\n\n| Step | Agent | Status | Highlight |\n|-------|--------|--------|----------|\n| 1 Triage | 🔍 Triager | ⏭️ | feature/refactor in the `simple` tier — the triager does not run |\n| 2 Exploration | 🧭 Explore | ⏭️ | not in this tier; the map came from `index_recall` + direct reads |\n| 3 Architecture | 📐 Architect | ⏭️ | not in this tier; the brief was already a stage-by-stage spec |\n| 4 Implementation | ⚙️ Coder | ✅ | 3 stages + docs, 27 files: window resolver, bounded watch loop, registration/status/MCP visibility, `queue.keepAwake` |\n| 5 QA | 🛡️ QA-Guardian | ⏭️ | not in this tier |\n| 6 Verification | ✅ Verifier | ✅ | 1567/1568 green, 0 fail; `-d` absent from every caffeinate argv; no test runs the real binary |\n| 6.5 Runtime | 📱 Runtime | ✅ | real 2-minute window on the operator's macOS, throwaway home, every acceptance point observed |\n| 7 Commit/PR | 🚀 Commit/PR | ✅ | `feat/windowed-runner` → https://github.com/maykonVinicius/nightshift/pull/65 |\n\nLessons saved: 0\n\n| Step | Agent | Status | Summary | Time |\n|-------|--------|--------|--------|-------|\n| 4a | ⚙️ Coder | ✅ | Stage 1: `src/queue/window.mjs`, the bounded watch loop, the five refusals, `detachedArgs` forwarding | 21m52s (measured across the coder lanes) |\n| 6a | ✅ Verifier | ✅ | Stage 1 gate: 1533 tests green | 4m22s (measured across the verifier lanes) |\n| 4b | ⚙️ Coder | ✅ | Stage 2: `window` in the registration, status line, JSON/MCP, the waiting-for-window hint | - |\n| 6b | — | ✅ | Stage 2 gate run inline: 1547 tests green | - |\n| 4c | ⚙️ Coder | ✅ | Stage 3: `queue.keepAwake`, the two caffeinate holds, the doctor row | - |\n| 4c' | ⚙️ Coder | 🔁 | Gap I caught: a bare `queue run --foreground` took no keepAwake hold; `runCycle` now holds as the top-level entry | - |\n| 4d | ⚙️ Coder | ✅ | Docs: `docs/queue.md`, `docs/cli.md`, README, CHANGELOG | - |\n| 6c | ✅ Verifier | ✅ | Final: 1567/1568 green + the three targeted greps | - |\n\n**Total:** ⏱️ 1h16m\n\n### 🎯 Objective met\n\n**Expected outcome:** the runner works only inside its window, finishes its job at `until`, exits 0 with its registration gone; the window is visible everywhere; `caffeinate` lives and dies with the runner; hermetic tests; `npm test` green.\n\nProved on the operator's macOS at level 4 (real runtime, throwaway home — the operator's own home was never touched):\n\n- waiting: `runner: running (pid 43067, watch every 5 s · window 21:50-21:52 · opens in 1m, foreground, …)`, claiming nothing;\n- registration carrying `\"window\": { \"from\": \"…T01:50:00.000Z\", \"until\": \"…T01:52:00.000Z\" }`;\n- `pgrep -lf caffeinate` → `43069 /usr/bin/caffeinate -s -w 43067`;\n- exit code 0 at 21:52:00, by itself, last line `window closed at 21:52`;\n- the registration directory empty afterwards and caffeinate 43069 gone with it.\n\nPlus 1567/1568 tests green (36 added), `-d` never in any argv, no test touching the real binary.\n\n### Findings to carry forward\n\n- Proposed decision 35: *A watch window is one-shot: the runner dies at `until`, recurrence belongs to the OS, and every live runner keeps the machine awake* — recorded as `proposed`; accept or reject it with `decision_update`. It was saved naming decision 3 as unrelated (3 deferred the window, it replaces none of its rules).\n- Two commits on the branch: the second one (`18f2ef6`) adds `test/mcp/server.test.mjs`, which I left out of the first commit's file list by mistake. Caught and pushed before the report; the branch is up to date with origin.\n- The worktree was kept, not deleted: per decision 27 the runner removes it at finalize on a `done` run, and `ExitWorktree` was still checking the pre-rename branch name.\n\n## Notice\n\n✅ Delivered — the queue can now be told to work only between two times, and the machine stays awake while it does\n\nWhat was missing: there was no way to say \"run the queue from 22:00 until 04:00, then stop\" — a watcher ran forever and a batch stopped as soon as the queue emptied. And on a Mac the laptop would fall asleep under a live run, including one waiting hours for a usage limit to reset, so an overnight run could quietly die halfway.\n\nWhat was done: a run can now be given a start and an end time. Before the start it is alive but takes no work; at the end it stops taking new work, lets the job it is already running finish to the end, and then shuts itself down, saying how many jobs it is leaving behind. The end time never cuts a job short. While any run is alive the machine is kept from falling asleep, and so is each job while it works. Both are visible: the queue view now shows the window and how long until it opens or closes, and it no longer promises a job will be picked up when the only run on the machine is still waiting for its start time.\n\nHow it was validated: the full automated suite passed with no failures, and a real two-minute window was run on the operator's Mac end to end — the waiting state, the machine being held awake, the run ending by itself exactly on time, and everything it had started being cleaned up with it.\n\nStill open — a DIFFERENT problem, does not affect this delivery:\n• Making this happen every night automatically is a separate piece of work: the window runs once and never repeats itself, so a nightly schedule has to be set up at the operating-system level, which is already on the roadmap.\n• A design note was recorded for review and is waiting for a decision: whether to confirm the one-shot rule as a standing constraint.\n\nOne honest limit, stated in the documentation and in the health check: closing the lid with no external display still puts the machine to sleep, and nothing here can wake a machine that is already asleep — an overnight run needs the lid open.\n\nRecord: • PR https://github.com/maykonVinicius/nightshift/pull/65","total_cost_usd":27.231402899999992}
